                <h2>Why Distinguish Between Runnable and Profitable Ads</h2>
<p>A common mistake in daily Meta ad management: a creative with high impressions and clicks is assumed to be performing well.</p>
<p>In reality, <strong>runnable only means the creative is receiving traffic</strong>; <strong>profitable means it's generating positive returns</strong>. A creative burning $500/day with 3% CTR but 0% conversion rate delivers zero ROI.</p>
<p>This SOP helps operations teams quickly distinguish between these two dimensions during creative reviews.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 1: Set Review Timeframe and Data Sources</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Item</th>
<th>Requirement</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Review period</td>
<td>Last 7-14 days</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Minimum sample</td>
<td>At least 100 conversion events per creative</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Data source</td>
<td>Meta Ads Manager ad set level</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Required metrics</td>
<td>Spend, impressions, clicks, CTR, CPC, conversions, CPA, purchase value</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Note</strong>: If a creative hasn't reached minimum sample size, continue observing for 3-5 more days before making any judgment.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Step 2: Identify Runnable Creatives</h2>
<h3>Criteria</h3>
<p>Runnable means the creative <strong>consistently consumes budget and generates clicks</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>Daily impressions ≥ 500</li>
<li>7-day average CTR ≥ 1.5% (adjustable by category)</li>
<li>Daily spend ≥ 20% of target CPA</li>
</ul>
<h3>Checklist</h3>
<ol>
<li>Sort campaigns by impressions in Ads Manager</li>
<li>Flag the top 50% by impression volume</li>
<li>Cross-reference with CTR above category average</li>
<li>The intersection is your "runnable" list</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>Step 3: Identify Profitable Creatives</h2>
<h3>Criteria</h3>
<p>Profitable means the creative <strong>generates positive returns</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>ROAS ≥ target threshold (typically 1.5x or 2x)</li>
<li>CPA ≤ 120% of target CPA</li>
<li>Conversions ≥ 5 (sample threshold)</li>
</ul>
<h3>Checklist</h3>
<ol>
<li>Export conversion data at ad set level</li>
<li>Calculate actual ROAS for each creative</li>
<li>Sort by ROAS descending, flag top 30% as "high performers"</li>
<li>Verify CPA falls within acceptable range</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>Step 4: Cross-Analyze with the Four-Quadrant Method</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Quadrant</th>
<th>Runnable</th>
<th>Profitable</th>
<th>Action</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>A</td>
<td>✅</td>
<td>✅</td>
<td>Scale, maintain strategy</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>B</td>
<td>✅</td>
<td>❌</td>
<td>Optimize, reduce budget</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>C</td>
<td>❌</td>
<td>✅</td>
<td>Test optimization, likely audience issue</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>D</td>
<td>❌</td>
<td>❌</td>
<td>Pause, redesign creative</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Focus on Quadrant B</h3>
<p>This is where most problems occur. "Runnable but not profitable" creatives typically show:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>High CTR, low CVR</strong>: Users click but aren't converted on landing page</li>
<li><strong>Creative-to-landing mismatch</strong>: Inconsistent messaging creates expectation gaps</li>
<li><strong>Traffic quality issues</strong>: Wrong optimization objectives (AEO/VO) lead to poor audience targeting</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>Step 5: Optimize "Runnable but Not Profitable" Creatives</h2>
<h3>Decision Tree</h3>
<pre><code>Runnable but not profitable?
├─ High landing page bounce rate?
│  └─ Optimize LCP, check CTA placement, review checkout flow
├─ Long conversion path?
│  └─ Shorten funnel, consider Click-to-Messenger or Add-to-Cart optimization
├─ Audience too broad?
│  └─ Narrow interest targeting, use LAL for scaling
└─ Creative fatigue?
   └─ Update UGC style, revise first 3 seconds
</code></pre>
<h3>Action Checklist</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] A/B test new CTA button copy</li>
<li>[ ] Align landing page headline with creative's main value proposition</li>
<li>[ ] Reduce budget by 30% for underperforming creatives, monitor for 3 days</li>
<li>[ ] Identify common patterns in "profitable" creatives and apply to new designs</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>FAQ</h2>
<p><strong>Q: Can I judge a creative after only 3 days?</strong><br />
A: Wait at least 7 days with ≥ 5 conversions before making judgments. Cold start data is volatile and can be misleading.</p>
<p><strong>Q: What if all creatives are "runnable but not profitable"?</strong><br />
A: It's likely not a creative issue. Check landing pages and optimization objectives first: review funnel data, verify Pixel events, and assess whether bidding strategy is too aggressive.</p>
<p><strong>Q: Do video and image creatives use the same criteria?</strong><br />
A: Core metrics are the same, but CTR benchmarks can differ. Video CTR typically exceeds image CTR, so adjust "runnable" thresholds accordingly.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Conclusion</h2>
<p>The goal of creative reviews isn't finding "runnable" ads—it's <strong>consistently building a library of profitable creatives</strong>.</p>
<p>Schedule a weekly review session (e.g., Monday mornings) to execute this SOP and close the loop. Runnable is just the starting point. Profitable is the destination.</p>
